"""Chapter 4 drill items — federalism in practice: the three lists, state creation,
revenue allocation, federal character and local government."""


def q(s, o, i, sol):
    return (s, o, i, sol)


DRILL_4 = [
    q("A system in which power is divided between a central government and component units, each supreme in its own sphere, is called", ["a unitary system", "a federal system", "a confederation", "a parliamentary system"], 1, "A federal system divides power and neither level can abolish the other. A unitary system delegates from the centre, and a confederation makes the centre the creature of the states."),
    q("Nigeria adopted federalism partly because", ["the country is large and ethnically diverse", "it had a single ethnic group", "the colonial government had governed it as a unitary state", "it wanted to join a confederation"], 0, "Size and diversity are the standard reasons, together with the separate colonial administrations of north and south and the regional bargain of the nationalists."),
    q("Only the National Assembly may legislate on subjects contained in the", ["residual list", "concurrent list", "exclusive list", "local government list"], 2, "The exclusive list. The concurrent list is shared, the residual list belongs to the states, and there is no such thing as a local government list."),
    q("Which of the following is on the exclusive legislative list?", ["Education", "Agriculture", "Defence", "Markets"], 2, "Defence is exclusive, along with currency, foreign affairs, police and citizenship. Education and agriculture are concurrent, and markets are residual."),
    q("Which of the following is a concurrent legislative matter?", ["Currency and coinage", "Education", "Chieftaincy", "Foreign affairs"], 1, "Education is concurrent: both the federation and the states may legislate. Currency and foreign affairs are exclusive, and chieftaincy is residual."),
    q("Chieftaincy is a matter for the states because it is on the", ["residual list", "exclusive list", "concurrent list", "federal list"], 0, "Chieftaincy is residual and belongs to the states. Defence, police and citizenship are on the exclusive list."),
    q("Where a state law is inconsistent with a federal law on a concurrent matter, the state law is", ["void in its entirety", "void to the extent of the inconsistency", "supreme", "referred to the Supreme Court automatically"], 1, "Section 4(5) of the 1999 Constitution: the federal law prevails and the state law is void only to the extent of the inconsistency. The doctrine is called covering the field."),
    q("The doctrine applied when federal and state laws conflict on a concurrent matter is", ["ultra vires", "covering the field", "stare decisis", "judicial notice"], 1, "Covering the field: once the federation has legislated on a concurrent matter, a state law on the same matter yields to the extent of the inconsistency."),
    q("The number of states in Nigeria is", ["19", "30", "36", "37"], 2, "Thirty-six states, plus the Federal Capital Territory. Nineteen was the figure after 1976, thirty after 1991, and the FCT is not a state."),
    q("The Federal Capital Territory was created in", ["1967", "1976", "1991", "1996"], 1, "1976, when the decision was taken to move the capital from Lagos to Abuja. The move itself was completed in 1991."),
    q("The last creation of states in Nigeria took place in", ["1987", "1991", "1996", "1999"], 2, "1996, under Abacha, when six states were created to bring the total to thirty-six."),
    q("How many local government areas are there in Nigeria?", ["744", "768", "774", "776"], 2, "774. The other figures are the examiner's near misses."),
    q("A new state is created by", ["the President by executive order", "the National Assembly with a two-thirds majority of the states", "the state house of assembly", "the Supreme Court"], 1, "Section 8 requires a request supported by the people of the area, a two-thirds majority in each house of the National Assembly and in two-thirds of the state houses of assembly."),
    q("A new local government area is created by", ["the National Assembly", "the state house of assembly", "the local government council alone", "the Federal Executive Council"], 1, "The state house of assembly creates local governments, with the consent of the council concerned. States, by contrast, are created by the National Assembly."),
    q("The main reason for creating more states in Nigeria has been", ["to bring government closer to the people and satisfy minority demands", "to reduce the number of local governments", "to increase the size of each unit", "to weaken the federal government"], 0, "Successive creations have appealed to proximity and to minority fears of domination by a large region. The effect, intended or not, has been a stronger centre."),
    q("In 1967 the regions were replaced by", ["twelve states", "nineteen states", "four regions", "thirty states"], 0, "Gowon created twelve states in 1967, abolishing the regions. Nineteen came in 1976, thirty in 1991 and thirty-six in 1996."),
    q("The revenue of the federation is shared among the tiers of government by", ["the Central Bank of Nigeria", "the Federation Account Allocation Committee", "the Federal Inland Revenue Service", "the National Assembly"], 1, "FAAC allocates the Federation Account vertically among the federal, state and local governments and horizontally among the states."),
    q("The 13 per cent of oil revenue paid to the oil-producing states is based on the principle of", ["population", "equality of states", "derivation", "need"], 2, "Derivation: the share follows the place from which the revenue is derived. Population, equality and need are the other principles in the formula."),
    q("Which principle of revenue allocation favours the most populous states?", ["Derivation", "Population", "Equality of states", "Internal revenue effort"], 1, "Population, on the argument that a larger population has a larger need. Equality of states favours the smallest units instead."),
    q("The principle of equality of states in revenue allocation", ["gives every state an equal share", "gives the poorest states the largest share", "rewards a state that raises more tax", "pays the producing state"], 0, "Equality gives each state the same basic amount on the argument that every state has a minimum cost of government."),
    q("The federal character principle is intended to", ["make all states equal in size", "ensure that federal appointments reflect Nigeria's diversity", "guarantee each state a share of oil", "abolish the civil service"], 1, "Section 14 of the 1999 Constitution requires the composition of federal institutions to reflect the diversity of the country, so that no section is excluded."),
    q("A criticism of the federal character principle is that it", ["can sacrifice merit to a quota", "weakens the federal government", "abolishes state governments", "prevents revenue sharing"], 0, "The standard criticism is that a diversity rule can become a quota that overrides qualification. The standard defence is that it prevents exclusion."),
    q("Local governments in Nigeria derive their statutory revenue from", ["the Federation Account", "the state house of assembly", "the Central Bank", "foreign donors"], 0, "A statutory share of the Federation Account is the main source, supplemented by state allocations, internal revenue and grants."),
    q("Which of these services is provided by a local government council?", ["Defence of the country", "Collection of market tolls and rates", "Making treaties", "Coining money"], 1, "Markets, tolls, rates, sanitation and local roads are local government functions. Defence, treaties and coinage are federal."),
    q("The main problem of local government in Nigeria is", ["excessive financial autonomy", "inadequate finance and undue state control", "the absence of any functions", "too few councils"], 1, "Councils depend on allocations that arrive late and are supervised, and sometimes dissolved, by the state — the classic pair of weaknesses."),
    q("A federal system differs from a confederation in that in a federation", ["the states may secede at will", "the central government can act directly on citizens", "there is no constitution", "the centre is the creature of the states"], 1, "A federal centre has its own powers and acts directly on citizens; in a confederation the centre depends on the states and the states may leave."),
    q("Which of the following is a feature of Nigerian federalism?", ["A single constitution for the whole country", "Separate constitutions for each state", "The right of states to make treaties", "The absence of a Supreme Court"], 0, "Nigeria has one constitution, which divides power. States do not have separate constitutions, cannot make treaties, and the Supreme Court settles disputes between the levels."),
    q("The Supreme Court's role in Nigerian federalism is to", ["settle disputes between the federal and state governments", "appoint state governors", "create states", "collect federal revenue"], 0, "The Supreme Court has original jurisdiction in disputes between the federation and a state, or between states — one of the defining institutions of a federation."),
    q("Fiscal federalism refers to", ["the division of taxing and spending powers between the levels of government", "the creation of states", "the federal character principle", "the appointment of judges"], 0, "Fiscal federalism is the revenue side of the federal bargain: who taxes, who shares, and on what principle."),
    q("The argument for a strong federal government in Nigeria is that it", ["holds the country together and redistributes revenue to poorer states", "weakens the states", "abolishes local government", "ends the need for a constitution"], 0, "The centre is the only government with a country-wide mandate, and it redistributes revenue. Critics reply that it has become too strong."),
    q("The argument against a strong federal government is that it", ["concentrates power and makes the states dependent", "creates too many states", "weakens the army", "abolishes the courts"], 0, "The standard criticism is over-centralisation: states depend on the centre for revenue, and the federal government controls the police and the bulk of the taxes."),
    q("Which of the following subjects is on the concurrent list?", ["Citizenship", "Agriculture", "Chieftaincy", "Currency"], 1, "Agriculture is concurrent, along with education, health, electricity and industrial development. Citizenship and currency are exclusive, chieftaincy residual."),
    q("The exclusive list contains all of the following except", ["defence", "foreign affairs", "police", "markets and motor parks"], 3, "Markets and motor parks are residual, left to the states and their local governments. Defence, foreign affairs and police are exclusive."),
    q("The federal government's power to legislate for the peace, order and good government of the federation is found in", ["section 4 of the 1999 Constitution", "section 8", "section 14", "section 42"], 0, "Section 4 establishes the legislative powers of the National Assembly, including the exclusive and concurrent lists. Section 8 deals with state creation."),
    q("A state in Nigeria may legislate on a matter on the residual list because", ["the list is not assigned to the federation", "the Constitution expressly grants it", "the National Assembly delegates it", "the Supreme Court permits it"], 0, "Residual matters are those not named in the exclusive or concurrent lists, and they fall to the states by default rather than by an express grant."),
    q("Which of the following is a consequence of the creation of more states?", ["More administrative units and a larger share of federal revenue going to state administrations", "A smaller federal government", "The abolition of local government", "The end of revenue allocation"], 0, "Each new state adds an administration and a share of the Federation Account, which is why state creation is also a revenue question."),
    q("The Federal Capital Territory is administered by", ["a state governor", "a minister appointed by the President", "a local government chairman", "the Senate"], 1, "The FCT is administered by a minister appointed by the President, and it is represented in the National Assembly by a senator and representatives."),
    q("The Nigerian federation is described as “centripetal” because", ["power and revenue have moved towards the centre over time", "the states have become stronger", "the regions were restored", "local government has been abolished"], 0, "Centripetal means tending towards the centre. The abolition of the regions, the creation of more dependent states and the concentration of revenue are the evidence."),
    q("One difference between the 1963 and 1999 federal arrangements is that", ["the 1963 Constitution had regions while the 1999 Constitution has states", "the 1963 Constitution had no federal government", "the 1999 Constitution abolished the states", "the 1963 Constitution had a unitary system"], 0, "1963 had four regions and a federal parliament; 1999 has thirty-six states and the FCT. Both were federal, but the units and their number differ."),
    q("A boundary dispute between two states is settled by", ["the Supreme Court", "the National Assembly", "the President's directive", "the local government council"], 0, "Disputes between states go to the Supreme Court's original jurisdiction, which is one of the reasons a federation needs a final court."),
]
